---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../components/CaseSection.astro";
import * as stylex from "@stylexjs/stylex";
import { caseStudyStyles } from "../../styles/caseStudyStyles";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Apolloon Race Operations | Warre Snaet"
  description="How Apolloon runs a 24-hour relay without the cloud: three Electron laptops that each hold the full SQLite database and agree on every change by majority vote."
  kicker="Paid client project, 2025–2026"
  heading="Apolloon race operations"
  lead="I built the software for a 24-hour relay race. It runs on three laptops on the event's wired network, with no cloud service. Each laptop keeps the whole race in SQLite, and the three agree on every change by majority vote, so a laptop can die without losing a confirmed lap."
  meta={[
    { label: "Role", value: "Sole developer, built it end to end" },
    { label: "Stack", value: "React, TypeScript, Express, tRPC, SQLite, Electron" },
    { label: "Runtime", value: "Three Electron laptops, wired LAN, browser screens" },
  ]}
>
  <CaseSection label="Purpose" title="Running a race without the cloud">
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>For 24 hours, the software registers runners, runs the queue and the warm-up, records laps, fixes mistakes, shows the live race on TVs, and exports results to Excel and CSV. The timer records a lap by typing the runner's number or pressing Space. The people at the tables aren't technical, so a failing laptop must never need a decision from them, and no two laptops may ever disagree about the queue or the laps.</p>
    <ul {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.list, caseStudyStyles.factsList)}>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Three laptops</strong>Timing, the queue desk, and the warm-up post. Each one holds the full database, and changes can be made from any of their screens.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Browser screens</strong>TVs and extra laptops open a laptop's address in a browser and store nothing. When their laptop dies, they reopen the same page on another laptop within 2.5 to 8 seconds.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Live updates</strong>Screens get every change through a tRPC subscription over WebSocket.</li>
    </ul>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Runtime" title="A desktop app with its own backend">
    <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.architecture)}>
      <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowRow)}>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>LAN clients</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Operator and TV browsers</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>Show the live race and move to another laptop when theirs stops answering</span></div>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Laptop runtime</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Electron + local Node process</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>Bundles the React UI, the Express and tRPC API, and the static files</span></div>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Laptop storage</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>SQLite and a replication log</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>Every laptop holds the full race, and only the current leader writes new changes</span></div>
      </div>
      <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowArrow)}>↓ UDP broadcast discovery + Raft over WebSocket ↓</p>
      <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowRow)}>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Find</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Announcements every 2 seconds</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>Laptops find each other again when an address changes, and a laptop on its own lists the groups it can join</span></div>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Link</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Automatic linking</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>An empty laptop joins the group that holds runners within about five seconds. Linking two groups that both hold runners takes a button press</span></div>
        <div {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNode)}><small {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.metadataLabel, caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>Agree</small><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeContent)}>One leader, majority vote</strong><span {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.flowNodeDescription)}>The leader puts every change in order and confirms it once two of the three laptops store it</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Consistency" title="What a single write goes through">
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>A change made on any screen goes to the leader. The leader writes the change and its log entry in one SQLite transaction, sends the entry to the other laptops at once, and confirms it to the screen once a majority has stored it. Nothing ever needs merging, because only one laptop decides the order.</p>
    <ul {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.list)}>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Exactly-once retries</strong>Every change carries a request ID. If the leader dies while a change is on its way, the next leader applies it once.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>No lost laps</strong>Only a laptop that holds every confirmed change can win an election, so a takeover never loses confirmed data.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>No two leaders</strong>A leader that can't reach a majority for 1.5 seconds steps down. A laptop behind a broken cable stops saving instead of recording laps the others never see.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Catching up</strong>A laptop that comes back catches up from the leader. If it holds changes the group never confirmed, it backs up its database first and then installs a full copy from the leader.</li>
    </ul>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Failover" title="Automatic, by majority vote">
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>The leader sends a heartbeat every 150 ms. When a laptop hears nothing for 1.5 to 3 seconds, it checks whether an election could succeed and then asks the others for their votes. With two of the three laptops up, everything keeps working. With one left, nothing is saved until a second one is back, unless someone confirms that the others are gone for good. That's why the design needs three laptops: with two, losing either one stops saving.</p>
    <ul {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.list, caseStudyStyles.factsList)}>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Shared clock</strong>The laptops have no internet time, so each one measures its offset to the leader's clock every two seconds.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Replication is not backup</strong>Every laptop takes a SQLite backup every five minutes and checks it with <code>quick_check</code> and <code>foreign_key_check</code>. It keeps the last 48 scheduled backups, and a restore covers the whole group.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Version checks</strong>Laptops only link when their app version and database schema match.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}><strong {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.emphasizedFact)}>Cross-platform builds</strong>The same self-contained Electron app is packaged for Linux, Windows, and macOS.</li>
    </ul>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Testing" title="Tested on the real laptops">
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>The repository has about 45 test files, including end-to-end tests of a linked group and a simulation of the Raft algorithm. A failover benchmark measures how long screens take to move to another laptop. A rehearsal script runs on the actual event laptops: it kills the app, cuts cables with firewall rules, and freezes laptops.</p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection label="Ownership" title="What I built">
    <p {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.paragraph)}>This is paid client work, so the code isn't public. I wrote all of it, from October 2025 on:</p>
    <ul {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.list)}>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>The Electron runtime, the typed API between browser and server, and the SQLite data model.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>The Raft group: elections, replication, catching up, automatic linking, discovery on the LAN, and browsers that move between laptops.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>The operator screens for registration, the queue and warm-up, timing, lap corrections, the live race, and exports.</li>
      <li {...stylex.attrs(caseStudyStyles.listItem)}>The tests, the Raft simulation, the failover benchmark, and the hardware rehearsal.</li>
    </ul>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudyLayout>
